DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261123T200000
UID:656ba4ecce5ca233900408854aa3e4dd@littlejumbobar.com
DESCRIPTION:Jack Wilkins writes music the way a field geologist keeps a no
 tebook. His records are named for the ground that made them. The Blue and 
 Green Project grew out of the culture and color of the Blue Ridge. The Ban
 ff Project and The Rundle Sessions came from a Fulbright year at the Unive
 rsity of Calgary and residencies at the Banff Centre in the Canadian Rocki
 es. Acadian Rhythms came from his term as composer in residence at Acadia 
 National Park\, on the granite coast of Maine. One album is simply called 
 Ridge Lines. The tenor saxophone carries all of it\, a big\, broad-shoulde
 red sound that has led his University of South Florida ensembles\, where h
 e has directed jazz studies since the 1990s\, to Montreux and the North Se
 a Jazz Festival.\n\nThis Monday brings him back toward the mountains he st
 ...
LOCATION:Little Jumbo: 241 Broadway St.\, Asheville\, NC 28801
URL:https://www.littlejumbobar.com/events/2238/jack-wilkins-quintet
X-SOURCE:Little Jumbo
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AVAS - The Acoustic Vibration Appreciation Society
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261124T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261124T200000
UID:2ebe0ee42e6b43134f541cf71a0bd023@littlejumbobar.com
DESCRIPTION:The Acoustic Vibration Appreciation Society starts from a simp
 le premise: sound was here first. Strings\, air\, and resonating wood were
  moving long before anyone named the motion music\, and AVAS treats that a
 s a starting point rather than a slogan. The band began as a friendship. J
 ay Sanders and Jason Krekel\, who had taken guitar lessons from Sanders\, 
 played their first gig together in 1991 at a student club at Hume-Fogg in 
 Nashville. By the mid-nineties both had found their way to Boone and into 
 the orbit of Andy Pond\, whose banjo braids three-finger rolls\, single-st
 ring runs\, and clawhammer frailing into a single voice. In 1999 the trio 
 recorded sixteen tracks live in West Asheville\, in a studio where racks o
 f clothing stood in for sound baffles\, and the record that came out of th
 ose s...
LOCATION:Little Jumbo: 241 Broadway St.\, Asheville\, NC 28801
URL:https://www.littlejumbobar.com/events/2337/avas-the-acoustic-vibration
 -appreciation-society
X-SOURCE:Little Jumbo
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Steve LaSpina New Music Ensemble
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261130T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261130T200000
UID:416d8efd8701f4303d7ec379a6508aa2@littlejumbobar.com
DESCRIPTION:Steve LaSpina learned the bass from his father in Wichita Fall
 s\, Texas\, and had to wait until he was tall enough to reach the top of t
 he upright. His father and grandfather both played in dance bands. The son
  carried that inheritance to Chicago\, where he was working professionally
  by fifteen\, and then to New York\, where he has spent more than forty ye
 ars in the company of jazz history. Marian McPartland heard him early. Ben
 ny Carter built his last working rhythm section around him after a single 
 rehearsal. Jim Hall kept him in his trio for years\, and anyone who knows 
 Hall's playing knows what that partnership asked of a bassist: patience\, 
 space\, and a sense of time that never had to announce itself.\n\nThat his
 tory matters on this particular Monday\, because LaSpina arrives with two 
 guit...
LOCATION:Little Jumbo: 241 Broadway St.\, Asheville\, NC 28801
URL:https://www.littlejumbobar.com/events/1816/the-steve-laspina-new-music
 -ensemble
X-SOURCE:Little Jumbo
END:VEVENT
